<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pamplona</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Iruña, Pompaelo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pamplona is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, in a basin at the foot of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> around 75 BCE, it became in the 9th century the seat of the Basque kingdom of Pamplona under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inigo-arista">Íñigo Arista</a>, later called the Kingdom of Navarre, which was allied by marriage with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a>. It is known for its cathedral, its citadel and the San Fermín festival with its running of the bulls.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pamplona.jpg" alt="Pamplona" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Luis Filpo Cabana · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monumento_a_los_Fueros_(Pamplona).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
